<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
    The shell's one theme: the platform's light Material theme, which is what
    the manifest named directly before this file existed, plus one attribute.

    android:forceDarkAllowed=false opts the whole window out of force dark
    (API 29+). Go draws every colour itself, so the platform must not recolour
    any of it. With force dark on (the developer override, or a vendor's
    system-wide dark mode: MIUI sets debug.hwui.force_dark) HWUI re-tints draw
    calls by heuristic, and on Compose's output it lightens dark text inside
    light Cards until it vanishes.

    Why the theme and not only View.setForceDarkAllowed on the decor view
    (MainActivity keeps that line too): ViewRootImpl reads this attribute to
    decide whether the window's renderer applies force dark at all. The
    per-view flag only asks HWUI to skip a subtree while it walks the render
    node tree, and on a Mi Max 3 (Android 10) that walk stopped honouring it
    once lesson 4.12's osmdroid MapView (an AndroidView) had been attached:
    every later lesson drew its Cards' text faded. A switch on the renderer has
    no tree to walk.

    tools:targetApi because the attribute is API 29 and minSdk is 24. Older
    releases ignore an unknown theme attribute, and they have no force dark.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.GrMob" parent="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:forceDarkAllowed" tools:targetApi="q">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>
